DECnet-Term for the Amiga
=========================

dnterm.device lets an Amiga terminal program log in to VAX/VMS and
other DECnet hosts over the network, with CTERM (the protocol behind
VMS "SET HOST"). The terminal program sees a modem on a serial port:
it dials a node name instead of a phone number.


Requirements
------------
- DECnet for the Amiga, installed and running (LIBS:decnet.library,
  dnetd)
- a terminal program that can use another device than serial.device,
  e.g. Term or NComm
- on the host: a CTERM server (VMS has one built in)


Installation
------------
Double-click "Install-DECnet-Term". It copies dnterm.device to DEVS:.
From the Shell: cd to this drawer, then

  Installer Install-DECnet-Term

To remove it, start Install-DECnet-Term again and choose "Remove".


Using it
--------
In the terminal program's serial settings, choose the device
dnterm.device and unit 0 instead of serial.device. Baud rate, parity
and handshake do not matter. Then type in the terminal window:

  ATD VAX1        log in to the node VAX1 (a name from your node
                  list ENV:DECnet/nodes)
  ATD 1.10        the same with the node's address

"CONNECT" means the link is up, and the host asks for user name and
password. When you log out, the device answers "NO CARRIER". A phone
book entry works too: give the node name as the phone number; ATDT and
ATDP in front of it are fine.

Units 0 to 3 are separate lines: to keep two sessions open at the same
time, start a second terminal program on unit 1.


Modem commands
--------------
  ATD node   connect (any key while it dials stops it)
  +++        back to command mode, the link stays up: one second
             without typing before and after it
  ATO        back online after +++
  ATH        hang up (also: closing the device)
  ATZ        hang up, settings back to E1 Q0 V1
  ATE0/ATE1  command echo off/on
  ATV0/ATV1  results as numbers/words
  ATQ0/ATQ1  results on/off
  ATI        version

Other AT commands are accepted and ignored, so modem init strings do
no harm. Results: OK (0), CONNECT (1), NO CARRIER (3), ERROR (4),
NO DIALTONE (6, DECnet is not running), BUSY (7), NO ANSWER (8). A
short reason comes before ERROR or NO CARRIER, e.g. "Unknown node" or
"Node has no CTERM server".

The carrier signal follows the link, so terminal programs show the
online time and notice when the host logs you out.


On VMS
------
CTERM tells VMS the terminal type VT200, but VMS does not treat the
line as a screen terminal until the terminal itself has answered.
Full-screen programs (EVE, MONITOR, ...) need this command after the
login first:

  $ SET TERMINAL/INQUIRE

It is best placed in your LOGIN.COM. The terminal program answers the
inquiry, so its emulation should be set to VT100 or VT220/ANSI.

The line editing keys of the VMS command line (cursor keys, Delete,
Ctrl-U, Ctrl-H, Ctrl-E) and Ctrl-Y work as on a VT terminal.


Tested
------
Term 4.8 on AmigaOS 3.1 (Amiberry), with OpenVMS 7.2 hosts: login,
DCL, EVE and MONITOR. Reports with other terminal programs and hosts
are welcome.


